/-!
# The Berthelot–Ogus décalage on an affine chart

Let `X` be a ringed space, let `I ⊆ 𝒪_X` be an invertible ideal sheaf and let `F^•` be a complex
of `I`-torsion-free `𝒪_X`-modules. On an open `V` on which `I` is generated by a single section
`g`, with image `f ∈ 𝒪_X(V)`, the sections over `V` of the sheaf-theoretic décalage `η_I F^•` of
Cohomology, Tag 0GT2 form the ordinary décalage `η_f` of the complex of sections `F^•(V)`, in the
sense of More on Algebra, Tag 0F7N. For a scheme `X` and the ideal sheaf of an effective Cartier
divisor this is the second assertion of Tag 0F8M.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.sectionsComplex`: the complex of sections of a complex of sheaves of
  modules over an open subset.
* `Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.generatorSection`: the image in `𝒪_X(V)` of a section of the ideal
  sheaf over `V`; this is the Cartier equation `f`.
* `Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.idealPowerSectionsMap`: the generator-normalized comparison from the
  sections of an integer ideal power to the localization of the sections at `f`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.etaSectionsIso`: the canonical isomorphism between the sections of
  `η_I F^•` over `V` and the décalage `η_f (F^•(V))`.
* `Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.etaSectionsIso_hom` characterizes it degreewise: composed with the
  inclusion of the décalage term into the localization, it is the normalized ideal-power
  comparison applied to the first projection of the defining fibre product.
* `Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.idealPowerSectionsMap_zero`, `idealPowerSectionsMap_succ` and
  `idealPowerSectionsMap_range` pin that comparison down: in degree `0` it is the canonical map
  into the localization, it is compatible with the inclusions of consecutive powers, and its
  image in degree `n` is the submodule `f^n F(V)`.
* `Stacks.Divisors.Eta.etaSectionsIso`: the second assertion of Tag 0F8M, for an effective
  Cartier divisor on a scheme and an affine open on which the divisor is cut out by `f`.

## Implementation notes

The comparison is built out of the trivialization of `I` by its generator. Tensoring a section
of `M` over `V` with `g` is bijective on the sections over `V`: on each stalk over `V` the
generator spans the stalk of `I`, so tensoring with it is bijective there by an elementary
computation with a cyclic module, and a family of maps on the opens contained in `V` which is
bijective on those stalks is bijective on the sections over `V`. This is where the hypothesis
that `g` generates `I` throughout `V` — rather than merely on `V` itself — is used. Dividing by
the generator then identifies the sections of consecutive ideal powers, and iterating the
identification from degree `0` gives the comparison with the powers of `f` inside the
localization. The two décalage terms are the corresponding fibre products, and the comparison of
the two squares gives the isomorphism, compatibly with the differentials.

Neither quasi-coherence nor affineness enters the argument, so the statement proved for a
ringed space is more general than the clause of Tag 0F8M; the two hypotheses of the source are
nevertheless carried by `Stacks.Divisors.Eta.etaSectionsIso`, so that the tagged statement reads
as the source does.

Several spellings of the ring of sections `𝒪_X(V)` are in play: the `CommRingCat`-valued
`X.presheaf.obj (op V)`, which the localization calculus of `EtaComplex` uses, and the
`RingCat`-valued
`(Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf ⟨X.presheaf, X.IsSheaf⟩).obj.obj (op V)` — the spelling
the décalage itself carries — over which the sheaves of modules are
indexed. These are the same ring, but identifying them unfolds `CategoryTheory.sheafCompose`,
which does not unfold at the transparency at which instances are synthesized. `sectionsFunctor`
and `generatorSection` are therefore plain definitions rather than abbreviations, so that the
spelling they declare is the one every statement below carries, and the instances
`moduleTensorStalkSections` (of `EtaSheaf`) and `moduleSectionCommRingCat` supply by hand the
module structures that instance search cannot find across the identification.

The generic helpers `local_sections_bijective_of_stalk_bijective`, `integerLinearEquiv` and
`pullbackSubmoduleIso` are stated for a sheaf of abelian groups, an integer-indexed family of
modules and a fibre product of modules respectively; nothing about the décalage enters them.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0F8M](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0F8M)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0GT2](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0GT2)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0GT5](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0GT5)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0F7N](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0F7N)
-/

